# Commits that changed formatting and nothing else. `git blame` should skip them.
#
# Turn it on locally (git does not read this file by default):
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# GitHub reads it without any configuration.
#
# Add one full 40-character SHA per line, with a comment above it saying what the commit did. A SHA
# belongs here ONLY if the commit is formatting and nothing else — see below for why that matters.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The 2026-08-21 Prettier reformat is deliberately NOT listed here. Do not add it.
#
# The plan for that change asked for the reformat to be its own commit so that its SHA
# could go in this file. That did not happen: the 118-file reformat and the whole lint-and-gates
# change landed together as one commit, which was pushed before the
# split. Rewriting published history was considered and declined.
#
# `blame.ignoreRevsFile` works per COMMIT, not per file, so listing that SHA would skip it everywhere.
# For the 118 files whose diff really is pure formatting that is exactly right. For the 13 files where
# the same commit made real changes -- a generator's modules, two runtime files, tsconfig.json,
# package.json and the configs -- blame would fall through to the commit BEFORE those lines existed. Attributing a
# real change to a commit that predates it is worse than noise: it is
# confidently wrong, and the generator is precisely where blame gets used.
#
# The cost of leaving it out, measured: 3,477 of the 38,144 lines in those 118 files (9%) are attributed
# to that commit. `git blame -w` recovers 23 of them, so it is not a workaround. `git log --follow` and
# `git log -S<string>` are unaffected and remain the better tools for tracing a line's real history.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
